The module must export exactly `{ viewportProgress }`. The function accepts a plain object with finite numeric properties `offset`, `viewportSize`, and `contentSize`. All three must be non-negative. It returns a new object `{ ratio, atEnd }`. If `contentSize <= viewportSize`, return `{ ratio: 1, atEnd: true }`. Otherwise, clamp `offset` into the scrollable interval from zero through `contentSize - viewportSize`; `ratio` is the clamped offset divided by that interval and `atEnd` is true exactly when the clamped offset reaches the interval's end. Extra properties are ignored. Invalid input throws `TypeError`, and input is never mutated.

Known-bad source:

```javascript
function viewportProgress(metrics) {
  if (metrics === null || typeof metrics !== 'object' || Array.isArray(metrics)) {
    throw new TypeError('metrics must be an object');
  }
  const { offset, viewportSize, contentSize } = metrics;
  if (![offset, viewportSize, contentSize].every(Number.isFinite) ||
      offset < 0 || viewportSize < 0 || contentSize < 0) {
    throw new TypeError('metrics must be finite non-negative numbers');
  }
  if (contentSize <= viewportSize) return { ratio: 1, atEnd: true };

  const maximum = contentSize - viewportSize;
  const clamped = Math.min(offset, maximum);
  return {
    ratio: clamped / contentSize,
    atEnd: clamped === maximum
  };
}

module.exports = { viewportProgress };
```

